A||||3||grinthock||A/C Question ---- Non Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||06-14-2001||02:20 PM||||Hey everyone hope someone can help me.  I have a 1995 Ford Escort Wagon POS, 1.9, I have A/C it's my daily driver....  The A/C is not getting as cold as it used to... I do'nt think this car is R-12, I don't know much about A/C (Other than it's hard on HP and I can't live without it!)<P>IT just seemsed today that it's not getting as cool....  <P>1)  Can I recharge this thing?<P>2)  Does the A/C Compressor clutch enable when I put my foot to the floor?||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||06-14-2001||02:32 PM||||Your Escort is likely R134...if in question, take a look under the hood...there should be a decal that indicates R134 and the proper charge amount.<P>If there's no decal, the type of fittings should tell you. R134 has quick-connect fittings, whereas R12 had the old screw-on Shrader fittings.<P>I can't tell you to just put in a can and see how it works. Find someone with a gauge set and hook it up...if it's low, you'll be able to tell from the readings.<P>And be sure your condenser is clean...get the bugs out and straighten any bent fins.<P>R134 is a different animal than R12...overfilling can result in poor performance just like being undercharged. And an R134 system operates at higher pressures, so there's more risk in servicing it yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||06-14-2001||02:34 PM||||So basically I should just take it somewhere to have it checked out then?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||06-14-2001||03:10 PM||||And to answer your question regarding the compressor clutch.<BR>Yes some cars with smaller pissamp engines have a switch that will turn off the compressor when you floor it. Call it emergency power  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
